Monday 7/20/2026
    - sketch of what we are working on
    - polish the secondary tasks from last week, watch videos, find python tutorial (get python experience)

    NOTES:
    - PLL = phase-locked loop. VCO = Voltage Controlled Oscillator. Booster does RF Sweep... goes from 36 to 53 MHz over 33ms.. Limited by the bandwidth of the I2C bus.. time budget ... we use 6 sig figs
    - Output = 25MHz * PLLMultiplier/d/rdiv (rdiv is always 1 in our case)
    - For our sakes we should just bound below by 500 kHz... and use powers of 2
    - Favor 900MHz to make it deterministic (so if we ran it multiple times we should get the same output multiple times)
    - d = 900/target (roughly the quotient)
    - VCO = d * target (what happens when you multiply the approx. quotient and target ... should be in 600-900 MHz range)

    ...
    - eventually we want to be able to see if we can discern between small gaps like 30.5 vs 30.6? And can we blindly test a min/max? 
    Pi Pico PIO.. multichannel

    Notes about what the signal generator actually does:
    - 30.5 MHz is the frequency used for the FAST/IOTA. RF frequency of the beam in fast/iota.  in BPMs (beam position monitors) or any instruemnt we want to give reference signals to start the chain of digital signals... boosters and main injector ramps from 36-53 and 53.6....-53.6....... the machines we are targeting are BPMs for FAST/IOTA... PIO we will use to target slow frequencies (SI5351 favors high frequencies)... "bit-banging" basically turning GPIO pin high and low at 10Hz or other low frequencies
    - clock board attached to pico board, which is attached to a trigger.. ADC = analog-digital converter, DAC = digital-analog converter. no DAC but there is ADC :D

